<h2><a name="4">Why Isn't My Favorite Author/Book Here?</a></h2>
It probably has something to do with this simple fact: <i>most</i>
of the SF titles ever written aren't here yet. Even using the most 
optimistic estimates, we have maybe 10 percent of the novels,
and about 5 percent of the short fiction in the genre (<i>using 
pessimistic estimates, we have about 4 percent of the novels, and
2 percent of the short fiction</i>.) So the odds that any given SF
title is here are fairly low.
<p>
This database evolved from an older awards database, so titles that
have been nominated, or have won, a major award will be here. Likewise,
the first concerted effort at filling in author bibliographies 
centered on those authors already present in the database - so
award nominated authors are more likely to have a reasonably filled-in
bibliography. This means that the database currently leans heavily
toward post-1953 titles, with the inception of the first of the 
major awards (the Hugo). So the odds are pretty good that most of 
the high quality fiction of the last 40 years is here.

<h2><a name="5">Why Is This Author/Book Here?</a></h2>
Some people get really hung up on labels and taxonomy, and might
get a little upset about the inclusion of a specific book or author
in the database which they feel is decidedly non-SF. We take the
right-wing approach to bibliographic justice: we'd rather incarcerate
an innocent mainstream book than let a guilty genre-related book
get away. Some authors permanently flit about the edges of the
genre (like Karen Joy Fowler, or Joyce Carol Oates), while others
seem to occasionally stumble into the genre (like Kafka, Flannery
O'Conner, or Faulkner).  So here's how we decide whether something
goes into the database or not:
<ol>
  <li>If a work of fiction has been nominated for a genre award, it
  goes in the database. In this case, someone else has decided that the
  work is suitably SF. This is why you'll find E. L. Doctorow's
  <i>Ragtime</i> in the database (?!?!).
  <li>If a work of fiction has been been published in a genre magazine,
  it goes in the database. How do we know a genre magazine when we see
  one? If it predominately publishes SF stories it's a genre magazine.
  Circular or not, that's the rule.
  <li>If the book in question has the words "Science Fiction", "Fantasy",
  or "Horror" printed on the spine, it goes into the database.
  <li>If the book in question doesn't have one of the magic words on
  its spine, but it is reviewed by a genre magazine, then it goes in
  the database. See above rule regarding genre magazines.
  <li>If the book or story has been previously listed in a well-known
  genre-related bibliography, it goes in the database.
  <li>Catch-all rule: if our gut-reaction tells us to put it in the
  database, it goes in the database.
  <li>If, based upon the above criteria, an author is determined to
  be a predominately genre-related author, then non-genre entries will
  be made to complete the author's bibliography, and are denoted as such.
  <li>If, based upon the above criteria, an author is determined to
  be a predominately mainstream author, then only genre-related entries
  will be made to the author's bibliography, while the rest are suitably
  jettisoned.
</ol>


