<h2><a name="8">Diskspace Requirements</a></h2>
Ahasuerus estimates that, within the SF genre, there are probably no
more than:

<pre>
        10,000 authors
       100,000 books
       250,000 short stories
</pre>

Armed with present disk usage requirements, an upward bound on
disk usage can be estimated:

<pre>
		Raw Data    Compiled Data
      ------------------------------------
      awards:	   1 Mb        1 Mb
      authors:     1 Mb        1 Mb
      titles:     26 Mb       31 Mb
      Pub Info:   15 Mb       10 Mb
      htmls:       2 Mb        2 Mb
      ------------------------------------
		  45 Mb   +   45 Mb  =  90 Mb
</pre>
So, several years from now, when all of the SF books, stories,
and zines ever written have been entered into the database, a 
compiled distribution would take about 45 Mb of disk space, and a
full distribution 90 Mb.
At the current rate of genre publication (~1700 titles/year), the
database would grow at a rate of about 0.7 Mb/year.
<p>
Note that the database is currently a long way from those kinds of disk
space requirements. We currently have an estimated 98% of the authors, 
12% of the books, and about 18% of the short fiction. Current 
requirements are about 13Mb.

